{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_db281344036e4d558de28f21e987c883","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The ASan runtime itself needs its shadow memory (0x10000000 = 256MB for shadow) which fails at low vlimits. So RLIMIT_AS below ~300MB makes ASan itself fail. These are ASan runtime failures, not the target bug. rc=134 (SIGABRT). These aren't the \"invalid buffer access\" we want — they're just ASan failing to init shadow due to vlimit too low.\n\nInteresting: at vlimit=500000, \"failed to allocate 0xdfff0001000\" — that's ASan shadow for huge... no that's the normal shadow region reservation failing.\n\nSo RLIMIT_AS approach collides with ASan's shadow. Not usable below ASan's ~750MB-1GB needs. Above that, real OOM (target allocations) won't happen since fonts are small.\n\nSo the OOM-forcing via RLIMIT isn't practical with ASan. The LD_PRELOAD approach is blocked by ASan interceptor precedence.\n\nLet me reconsider. Two options:\nA) Build a NON-ASan version for OOM exploration with LD_PRELOAD. Find the crash (SIGSEGV), then reconstitute under ASan and verify.\n\nB) Use ASan's own OOM-simulation. ASan supports `allocator_may_return_null` but only triggers when a single mmap allocation is larger than available; for GNU asan it also has `allocator_may_return_null` and can simulate with `ASAN_OPTIONS=...`. But to force \"every allocation fail after N\", ASan doesn't have that like a budget. Actually, ASan has `hard_rss_limit_mb` and `malloc_context_size`... None give deterministic point-failure.\n\nOption A is cleanest. Let me build a non-ASan version of the fuzzing library and standalone, then use LD_PRELOAD fail.so to sweep OOM failure points, find crashes, and validate.\n\nLet me reconfigure with no sanitizer and rebuild fuzzing lib. To avoid disturbing the ASan lib, I'll do a full cleaned reconfigure. Actually simpler: create a separate copy? The build system is in-tree. Let me just reconfigure without sanitizer (default gcc) and rebuild, then build standalone non-ASan. I can keep the ASan lib by copying it aside first.\n\nLet me copy the ASan .a and .la aside, then reconfigure clean non-ASan.\n\nActually, I'll just rebuild completely non-ASan for exploration. Let me copy the asan binary result first (already have hb-fuzzer and hb-fuzzer-standalone built as ASan). Keep those. Then clean reconfigure non-ASan.\n\nBut then I'd lose the ASan lib for later verification. I can always rebuild ASan again. Let me just switch to non-ASan now for exploration.\n\nLet me reconfigure without CFLAGS sanitizer and rebuild fuzzing lib + standalone non-ASan.\n\nActually, I dislike losing the ASan build. Let me copy the ASan standalone binary to safe name (it's ASan-instrumented, self-contained). hb-fuzzer and hb-fuzzer-standalone are ASan, static-linked libharfbuzz — good, keep them.\n\nNow reconfigure non-ASan and rebuild fuzzing lib.","signature":""}],"model":"DeepSeek-V4-Flash-0731","usage":{"input_tokens":66349,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"797f274e-a18b-426c-8154-8e32629cfe04","uuid":"3d49660b-1257-4c73-abc3-0ad50696f86c"}
