{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_58992f03589a4651a3cc26d75467df51","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"big95 (9.4MB) → 69MB RSS. Baseline 34MB. So bigger blob → more RSS even though most is ASan overhead/mapping.\n\nI want to push RSS as HIGH as possible within ≤10MB input under ASan. big95 is 9.4MB → 69MB. What if I make a maximal 10MB font that harfbuzz actually parses with max structure (maxed lookups)? That could push higher via allocations, not just mapping.\n\nLet me measure big256/big512 locally (they're >10MB but measure to understand scaling). big512 is 536MB — if RSS scales ~7x, that'd be ~3.7GB. But these can't be submitted (>10MB). Let me still measure one to understand the ASan memory multiplier.\n\nActually, more useful: craft a ~10MB font that ALSO maximizes internal allocations (65535 lookups etc.), and measure. That's the submission.\n\nBut first, let me reconsider the server's limit. big95 (9.4MB, 69MB RSS locally) → server exit 0. So server ran fine with ~equivalent. If server limit were ≥ ~100MB, big95 passes. To cross, I need RSS > server limit. Without knowing it, I push to the max ~10MB font can reach.\n\nLet me measure what a maximal 10MB font reaches. Two factors: (a) raw blob mapping, (b) internal allocations. The blob mapping dominates (ASan multiplies). A ~10MB font → maybe ~75-90MB RSS. Not huge. So unless server limit is ~80MB, this won't cross.\n\nHmm. This is precarious. But it might be the intended approach — the server likely has a fixed memory limit for this OOM task, and the answer is \"as large a font as possible to cross it.\"\n\nBut wait — the summary says I'm told the server has \"ample memory.\" And big95 exit 0. If ample, I can't cross with 10MB.\n\nLet me reconsider the whole premise ONE more time. \"An invalid buffer access occurs in out-of-memory (OOM) situations.\" \n\nMaybe the \"OOM situation\" in the harness is triggered NOT by process memory but by an internal harfbuzz notion. Actually — reconsider: harfbuzz has its OWN OOM handling via `hb_alloc` / `hb_malloc` returning NULL on failure being treated as \"in_error\" and graceful. The bug is that guess_segment_properties doesn't check. \n\nBut how is OOM induced in a normal run? Through a genuinely huge allocation request. What if a crafted font causes harfbuzz to request a HUGE single allocation (e.g., via a corrupted table length read as 32-bit that's not validated)? If such a request succeeds in local (ample) memory, no crash; but if it's so huge it fails even on the server (e.g., > 2^31 on 32-bit, or malloc returns NULL for absurd size under ASan with allocator_may_return_null)... Under ASan WITHOUT -allocator_may_return_null, a huge allocation causes ASan to ABORT (allocation-size-too-big). That's a crash! exit != 0! And it'd be a real bug triggering on my submitted input regardless of server memory limit.\n\nSo: find a table length in harfbuzz that is read from the font as an unbounded/32-bit value and used to size an allocation, WITHOUT validation against the blob size, with the allocation large enough to fail. But harfbuzz sanitizes tables and validates lengths. Historically there were such bugs. This is the \"invalid buffer access\" — but that's OOB not OOM.\n\nActually the cleanest real crash: a 32-bit length that gets read as huge and then a real read/write goes OOB → ASan heap-buffer-overflow. That IS \"invalid buffer access\". The description says \"in OOM situations\" but maybe the actual CVE is an OOB.\n\nI think I should just fuzz aggressively with libFuzzer to find a genuine ASan crash, per the explicit instructions. Let me start a serious background fuzz campaign with the valid TTF corpus as seeds. libFuzzer under ASan will find OOB/redzone issues. Let me set it up and run it.\n\nLet me generate a decent corpus of valid fonts as seeds, plus start from big95. Run libFuzzer with default rss, many iterations.\n\nLet me start a background fuzz job now (it can take a