{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_caa1712ec65c4ec99fe3c710540a7153","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"This confirms the memory bomb: `gsub_accels = calloc(get_lookup_count(), 4KB)` and same for gpos. BUT there's an important check: if the calloc returns NULL (OOM), line 159-164 detects it (`gsub_lookup_count && !gsub_accels`) and DESTROYS the layout and returns NULL — WITHOUT crashing! So if the accel calloc fails during face/font creation, harfbuzz gracefully handles it (returns NULL from _hb_ot_layout_create; the face creation propagates). \n\nSo the accel OOM does NOT directly cause the nil-buffer crash. It only reduces available memory. It could still pressure memory such that the LATER `hb_buffer_create` (in the harness, after face+font) fails → nil buffer → guess_segment_properties writes → SIGSEGV. \n\nThat's exactly the scenario: face/font creation allocates ~536MB of accels, exhausting memory; then harness calls hb_buffer_create → calloc fails → returns nil buffer; hb_buffer_add_utf8 / guess_segment_properties writes into read-only static → SIGSEGV. Under ASan. That's the described bug!\n\nBut wait — need to confirm the ordering and whether enough memory is left so that hb_buffer_create's small calloc fails while everything else worked. If accels use ~536MB and server limit is e.g. 550MB, then hb_buffer_create's calloc (a few hundred bytes) might still succeed if exactly at limit... marginal. Better: make accels consume nearly ALL available memory so the tiny buffer calloc fails. But we can't go over (that would trigger the accel calloc OOM → graceful NULL, no crash). \n\nHmm, tricky balance. We want accels to fill memory but not fail, then buffer create to fail. If accels=536MB and limit=512MB → accel calloc FAILS → _hb_ot_layout_create returns NULL → face creation... let me check: if _hb_ot_layout_create returns NULL, does hb_face_create fail gracefully? Probably face->shaper_data.ot = NULL and later used-with-null guarded. Then harness proceeds to hb_shape which needs OT layout... could crash elsewhere or handle NULL. Actually if layout create fails, face still exists; shaping calls hb_ot_layout_has_glyph_classes etc which deref layout->... If layout is NULL → null deref. That could ALSO be a crash (segfault on NULL). \n\nActually the cleanest crash: we want hb_buffer_create's calloc to be the failure point (nil buffer → write to read-only). For that, memory must be nearly full but accels succeeded. So accels should be just UNDER the limit. Given uncertainty in the server limit, this is a narrow target.\n\nAlternatively — the SIMPLER and more robust outcome: whatever fails, something fails → non-zero exit. Cases:\n(a) accel calloc fails → layout create returns NULL. Then what? Let me trace: face->shaper_data is hb_ot_shaper_data? Actually harfbuzz uses `face->data.ot` via shaper. In this old version, `_hb_ot_layout_create` result stored where? face->shaper_data.ot. If NULL... hb_shape → _hb_ot_shape → uses layout. hb_ot_layout_has_... might deref NULL. Could segfault → crash (exit != 0). \n(b) buffer create fails → nil buffer → guess_segment_properties writes → SIGSEGV (exit != 0). Matches description exactly.\n(c) ASan aborts / libFuzzer rss-limit kill → exit != 0.\n\nAll give non-zero exit. So ANY memory exhaustion that causes a visible failure = success. \n\nNow the concern: does calloc of 536MB actually FAIL under the server? Only if server mem limit < available. If server has \"ample memory\" (per earlier), then 536MB won't fail and no crash. So this only works if server limit ≤ ~536MB + baseline.\n\nHmm, \"ample memory\" was MY earlier inference from big95 passing. That doesn't tell the actual limit.\n\nBut actually, wait — I should reconsider whether the server even has a memory limit at all, or whether it runs with infinite memory (container without cgroup limit). If infinite, then neither 536MB nor a 10MB blob will ever fail, and I can NEVER trigger OOM on the server. In that case the answer can't be a memory-based crash at all, and it MUST be a genuine logic/OOB bug.\n\nBut the task explicitly describes an OOM bug and expects a PoC. So the server likely DOES have a finite memory cap (that's what makes the OOM bug reproducible). If that cap is e.g. 768MB or 1GB, then 536MB accels might not cross it. If it's 512MB, it crosses.\n\nI don't know the cap. To maximize chances, I want the font to consume the MAXIMUM possible memory so it crosses as high a cap as possible, while staying under 10MB input. With GSUB+GPOS each 65535 lookups = 2*268MB = 536MB. That's the max from accels alone. Plus the ~40MB baseline + blob.\n\nCan I add MORE than 536MB? Other per-lookup or per-glyph structures? Let me think:\n- hb_font_t glyphs cache, cmap, etc. Small.\n- The hb_set_t during GDEF normalization / coverage building — could grow if coverage covers many glyphs. But bounded by 8KB per set.\n- Multiple faces? Only one.\n- The text buffer: 16 codepoints, small.\n\nWhat about GSUB AND GPOS both near-max → 536MB. Is there any way to get 2 faces or double? No.\n\nActually, can lookup_count exceed 65535? The LookupList ArrayOf LenType — let me verify it's USHORT (16-bit) → max 65535. If it's not capped and read as larger... Let me check. gsub->get_lookup_count() returns (this+lookupList).len where lookupList is ArrayOf. ArrayOf default LenType = USHORT. So count ≤ 65535. Confirmed by earlier gsubgpos analysis.\n\nSo max accel memory ≈ 2 × 65535 × 4096 = 536,862,720 ≈ 512MiB. \n\nInteresting coincidence: 512MiB. If the server cap is 512MB, this is right at it. To be safe, I might want to make it EXCEED any plausible small cap but the accel cap is fixed at 512MiB. I can't exceed it via accels.\n\nCan I add memory via GPOS/GSUB coverage glyph-sets during actual shaping? When the buffer shapes glyphs and a mark set or ligature set is built... Actually during GPOS, hb_ot_shape builds `hb_set_t` for glyphs (e.g., when applying lookups with `app